A scheduled job that crashes at 7am fails silently unless you build in a way to notice.

```js
#!/usr/bin/env node
import 'dotenv/config';

try {
  await runSummary();
  console.log(`[${new Date().toISOString()}] Success`);
} catch (err) {
  console.error(`[${new Date().toISOString()}] FAILED: ${err.message}`);
  // Send yourself a notification — don't just log and hope you check
  await notifyFailure(err.message);
  process.exit(1);
}
```

At minimum: log every run with a timestamp, so you can check later whether it ran. Better: send yourself an alert (Slack message, email) only when it fails — you don't need a notification every time something works.

### What a webhook actually is

A webhook is just an HTTP POST request that another service sends to a URL you provide, when something happens on their end.

```js
import express from 'express';
const app = express();
app.use(express.json());

app.post('/webhooks/new-order', async (req, res) => {
  const order = req.body;
  await summarizeAndNotify(order);
  res.status(200).send('OK'); // acknowledge receipt quickly
});

app.listen(3000);
```

The service you're integrating with (Stripe, GitHub, a form tool) needs a public URL to send that POST to — during development, tools like `ngrok` expose your local server temporarily so external services can reach it.

A public webhook URL can be called by anyone who finds it — not just the real service. Signature verification confirms a request actually came from who it claims to.

```js
import crypto from 'crypto';

app.post('/webhooks/new-order', async (req, res) => {
  const signature = req.headers['x-webhook-signature'];
  const expected = crypto
    .createHmac('sha256', process.env.WEBHOOK_SECRET)
    .update(JSON.stringify(req.body))
    .digest('hex');

  if (signature !== expected) {
    return res.status(401).send('Invalid signature');
  }

  await summarizeAndNotify(req.body);
  res.status(200).send('OK');
});
```

Most real services (Stripe, GitHub) sign their webhooks with a secret you configure — check their docs for the exact header name and algorithm, but the pattern is always the same: compute the expected signature yourself, compare it to what arrived, reject a mismatch.

### Slack incoming webhooks — the simplest integration

Slack lets you create a unique URL for a channel; anything you POST to that URL appears as a message there. No OAuth, no bot setup needed for this use case.

**Setup** (one time, in Slack): Workspace settings → Apps → Incoming Webhooks → choose a channel → copy the URL.

```js
async function postToSlack(text, webhookUrl) {
  const res = await fetch(webhookUrl, {
    method: 'POST',
    headers: { 'Content-Type': 'application/json' },
    body: JSON.stringify({ text })
  });
  if (!res.ok) throw new Error(`Slack post failed: ${res.status}`);
}

await postToSlack('Daily feedback summary: 3 new items flagged for review.', process.env.SLACK_WEBHOOK_URL);
```

That's the entire integration — one POST request with a `text` field.

Slack supports a subset of markdown in the `text` field, and a richer block format for more control:

```js
async function postSummaryToSlack(items, webhookUrl) {
  const lines = items.map(i => `• *${i.title}*: ${i.summary}`).join('\n');
  const payload = {
    blocks: [
      { type: 'header', text: { type: 'plain_text', text: '📋 Daily Feedback Summary' } },
      { type: 'section', text: { type: 'mrkdwn', text: lines } },
      { type: 'context', elements: [{ type: 'mrkdwn', text: `Generated ${new Date().toLocaleDateString()}` }] }
    ]
  };
  const res = await fetch(webhookUrl, {
    method: 'POST', headers: { 'Content-Type': 'application/json' }, body: JSON.stringify(payload)
  });
  if (!res.ok) throw new Error(`Slack post failed: ${res.status}`);
}
```

`*bold*`, `_italic_`, and `• ` bullets all render correctly in `mrkdwn` fields. Blocks let you separate a header from content from a timestamp, which reads far better in a busy channel than one long paragraph.

### Sending an email with Resend

```bash
npm install resend
```

```js
import { Resend } from 'resend';
const resend = new Resend(process.env.RESEND_API_KEY);

async function sendSummaryEmail(to, summary) {
  const { data, error } = await resend.emails.send({
    from: 'reports@yourdomain.com',
    to,
    subject: 'Your daily summary',
    text: summary, // plain text fallback
    html: `<p>${summary.replace(/\n/g, '<br>')}</p>`
  });
  if (error) throw new Error(`Email failed: ${error.message}`);
  return data;
}
```

The `from` address needs to be on a domain you've verified with your email provider — you can't send as an arbitrary address without that setup, which is a deliberate anti-spam measure.

Email delivery can fail for reasons entirely outside your code: invalid address, provider rate limit, domain reputation issue.

```js
async function sendWithRetry(to, summary, maxAttempts = 3) {
  for (let attempt = 1; attempt <= maxAttempts; attempt++) {
    try {
      return await sendSummaryEmail(to, summary);
    } catch (err) {
      console.error(`Attempt ${attempt} failed: ${err.message}`);
      if (attempt === maxAttempts) throw err;
      await new Promise(r => setTimeout(r, 1000 * attempt)); // backoff
    }
  }
}
```

For a scheduled report, a failed send after retries should be logged clearly (or trigger a Slack alert, using what you built in the previous lesson) — a report that silently never arrived is worse than one that arrives late.

### A real pipeline: feedback triage

```
[Cron, daily 7am]
     ↓
Fetch new feedback entries from a database
     ↓
For each entry: Claude classifies as urgent/normal/spam
     ↓
Urgent  → post to Slack #urgent-feedback immediately
Normal  → collect into a daily digest
Spam    → discard, log count only
     ↓
[End of day] Send digest email of normal-priority items
```

Each arrow is a place where the previous lessons apply directly: the cron trigger, the Claude call with a classification prompt, the conditional routing in your own code, the Slack post, the email send.

```js
async function runTriagePipeline() {
  const entries = await fetchNewFeedback();
  const digest = [];
  for (const entry of entries) {
    const classification = await classify(entry, client); // 'urgent' | 'normal' | 'spam'
    if (classification === 'urgent') await postToSlack(`🚨 ${entry.text}`, SLACK_URL);
    else if (classification === 'normal') digest.push(entry);
  }
  if (digest.length > 0) await sendSummaryEmail(TEAM_EMAIL, formatDigest(digest));
}
```
